\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Exact decomposition of the copperplate-to-baseline cost gap into a loss main effect, a spatial-topology main effect and their interaction, for Memmingen (left) and as a distribution across the 135 synthetic networks (right). The additive identity closes to machine precision, so the three terms are measured rather than fitted.}}{24}{figure.caption.30}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:decomp}{{4}{24}{Exact decomposition of the copperplate-to-baseline cost gap into a loss main effect, a spatial-topology main effect and their interaction, for Memmingen (left) and as a distribution across the 135 synthetic networks (right). The additive identity closes to machine precision, so the three terms are measured rather than fitted}{figure.caption.30}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Estimation bias against decision regret for every level, as a percentage of the baseline operating cost. For the copperplate and the topology-only control the two carry \emph  {opposite signs}: a schedule that appears cheaper on paper is markedly more expensive to execute. Loss-aware levels show regret approximately equal to bias.}}{24}{figure.caption.32}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:regret}{{5}{24}{Estimation bias against decision regret for every level, as a percentage of the baseline operating cost. For the copperplate and the topology-only control the two carry \emph {opposite signs}: a schedule that appears cheaper on paper is markedly more expensive to execute. Loss-aware levels show regret approximately equal to bias}{figure.caption.32}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Zone-boundary choice against the effects it must be compared with, on a logarithmic cost axis. With the total heat-loss capacity held equal across all twenty-four partitions, the residual routing effect (11\,EUR) is two orders of magnitude below the spatial-topology main effect (961\,EUR) and three below the loss main effect (19\,737\,EUR).}}{24}{figure.caption.33}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:r16}{{6}{24}{Zone-boundary choice against the effects it must be compared with, on a logarithmic cost axis. With the total heat-loss capacity held equal across all twenty-four partitions, the residual routing effect (11\,EUR) is two orders of magnitude below the spatial-topology main effect (961\,EUR) and three below the loss main effect (19\,737\,EUR)}{figure.caption.33}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Fidelity design rule. The cost gap a copperplate misses (the loss burden) against the a-priori loss number $\lambda =\text  {annual loss}/\text  {annual demand}$. The parameter-free physics curve $b=\lambda /(1+\lambda )$ (navy) tracks the measured burden of the 135 synthetic networks (grey) and Memmingen (star) with $R^2=\textbf  {0.87}$. Shaded bands give the screening decision -- copperplate/adder, calibrate an adder, or resolve nodes -- read off from $\lambda $ alone, without solving.}}{24}{figure.caption.39}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:rule}{{7}{24}{Fidelity design rule. The cost gap a copperplate misses (the loss burden) against the a-priori loss number $\lambda =\text {annual loss}/\text {annual demand}$. The parameter-free physics curve $b=\lambda /(1+\lambda )$ (navy) tracks the measured burden of the 135 synthetic networks (grey) and Memmingen (star) with $R^2=\textbf {0.87}$. Shaded bands give the screening decision -- copperplate/adder, calibrate an adder, or resolve nodes -- read off from $\lambda $ alone, without solving}{figure.caption.39}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Drift of a frozen loss adder against trunk pipe length across the 135 synthetic networks. An adder calibrated on any one network mis-estimates the loss burden on others by a mean of 23.5 and up to 40.1 percentage points of cost, which is why the node-resolved model -- computing the loss endogenously -- is the transferable one.}}{25}{figure.caption.40}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:drift}{{8}{25}{Drift of a frozen loss adder against trunk pipe length across the 135 synthetic networks. An adder calibrated on any one network mis-estimates the loss burden on others by a mean of 23.5 and up to 40.1 percentage points of cost, which is why the node-resolved model -- computing the loss endogenously -- is the transferable one}{figure.caption.40}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Supply-temperature flexibility. Lowering the plant supply temperature reduces thermal loss but shrinks the temperature difference, so flow and pumping rise. The cost-optimal reduction is 17.5\,K; beyond 20\,K the pipe velocity limit binds, and hydraulics change from a negligible cost into the binding constraint.}}{25}{figure.caption.43}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:tsup}{{9}{25}{Supply-temperature flexibility. Lowering the plant supply temperature reduces thermal loss but shrinks the temperature difference, so flow and pumping rise. The cost-optimal reduction is 17.5\,K; beyond 20\,K the pipe velocity limit binds, and hydraulics change from a negligible cost into the binding constraint}{figure.caption.43}{}}
